  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "The length of a rectangle is increased by \\(10\\%\\) and the width is decreased by \\(10\\%.\\) What percent of the old area is the new area?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(90\\)",
    "B": "\\(99\\)",
    "C": "\\(100\\)",
    "D": "\\(101\\)",
    "E": "\\(110\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The new length is \\(110\\%\\) of the old length, and the new width is \\(90\\%\\) of the old width. Let the original positive length and width be \\(L\\) and \\(W\\). Since a rectangle's area is length times width, its new area is\n\\[\n(1.10L)(0.90W)=0.99LW.\n\\]\nThe old area is \\(LW\\), so the new area is \\(\\boxed{99\\%}\\) of the old area, choice B.",
